<section class="phero"><div class="phero-media"><img class="phero-img" src="/assets/img/photos/foursquare-snow.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/foursquare-snow-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/foursquare-snow.webp 1200w" sizes="100vw" width="1200" height="864" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" data-parallax="0.18"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><i aria-hidden="true">›</i><a href="/ct-appraisal-services/">Services</a><i aria-hidden="true">›</i><span aria-current="page">Ice Dam &amp; Winter Storm Appraisal</span></nav><p class="kicker kicker-light">Appraisal service</p><h1>Ice Dam and Winter Storm Claim Appraisal in Connecticut</h1><p class="phero-lede">When snow sits on a Connecticut roof for weeks, heat from the house melts it, the eaves refreeze, and water backs up under the shingles. Winters like 2011 and the February 2013 blizzard also brought outright collapses.</p><div class="btn-row"><a class="btn btn-coral" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ic" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-ghost-light" href="/contact-ct-appraiser/">Free claim review</a></div><dl class="hero-facts"><div><dt>Peak months</dt><dd>January–March</dd></div><div><dt>Notable</dt><dd>2011 roof collapses · 2013 blizzard</dd></div><div><dt>Often missed</dt><dd>Wet insulation</dd></div></dl></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose">
<h2>How ice dams cause interior damage</h2>
<p>An ice dam forms when the upper roof is warm enough to melt snow but the eaves are cold enough to refreeze it. Meltwater pools behind the ridge of ice and works its way under shingles, into the soffit, down the inside of exterior walls and through ceilings. Damage often appears far from where water entered.</p>
<h2>Snow load and collapse</h2>
<p>In January and February 2011, repeated heavy snowfalls caused roof failures on homes, barns and commercial buildings across Connecticut. The February 2013 blizzard dropped well over two feet on much of the state. Flat roofs, older barns and additions with undersized framing are the most vulnerable.</p>
<h2>What we scope</h2>
<ul>
<li>Interior ceiling, wall and floor damage along the path of the water</li>
<li>Wet insulation in attics and wall cavities, which is easy to miss and expensive to leave</li>
<li>Damaged soffits, fascia and gutters torn off by ice</li>
<li>Roof decking and shingle damage at the eaves, and ice-and-water shield required by code on replacement</li>
<li>Structural repairs and shoring after a partial collapse</li>
</ul>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Coverage nuance</b>Many policies cover interior water damage caused by ice dams while excluding the cost to fix the roof condition that allowed the dam, such as poor ventilation. Some carriers also cite wear and tear. Know which part of the claim is being disputed.</div>

<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Are ice dams covered by homeowners insurance?<svg class="ic chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Interior damage from ice dam leaks is commonly covered. Removing the ice and correcting ventilation or insulation problems may not be. Policy wording controls.</p></div></details><details><summary>Is a roof collapse from snow covered?<svg class="ic chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Collapse caused by the weight of ice or snow is commonly covered under homeowners forms, subject to policy terms and exclusions.</p></div></details><details><summary>Should I hire someone to remove ice dams?<svg class="ic chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Removing snow and ice safely is reasonable mitigation and the cost may be reimbursable. Avoid damaging shingles with picks or hammers, and keep receipts.</p></div></details></div>
